  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1153 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nToday - Friday: Light Precip Chances and Cooling Down\n\nMid to high level clouds have formed over much of the Upper \nMidwest early this morning. The increase in cloud cover is \nassociated with increasing mid level moisture out ahead of an \nH300 trough digging southeast out of the Dakotas. The moisture \nalong with lift provided by the trough should develop \nprecipitation across portions of the area early this morning \ninto the afternoon. Given the progressive nature of the trough \nand forecast atmospheric profiles not showing deep layer \nmoisture, especially in the lower levels near the surface, only \nvery light rain is anticipated if any showers develop over the \narea. Forecast rainfall amounts continue to support this trend \nwith most areas very likely staying below a tenth of an inch of \naccumulation. The best chances for rainfall will be in the \nmorning, however secondary waves may bring additional chances \nduring the afternoon into early Thursday morning. If any showers\ncan develop early Thursday morning, some snow flurries may mix \nin with the rain since strong CAA will push south over the Great\nLakes region. No snow accumulation is expected since the depth \nof the moisture will be even less than this morning.\n\nSpeaking of the colder air, H850 temperatures dipping down to \naround -5 to -10 Celsius will intrude over the region from \nThursday through at least the first half of the weekend. With \nthe much cooler airmass in place, high temperatures will almost \ncertainly fall into the 30s and 40s for Thursday and Friday. \nOvernight lows even have the potential to drop down in the low \n20s on Friday night. There is at least medium confidence \n(50-70%) for locations across MN and west central WI to stay \nbelow 40 on Friday and that potential has been increasing, which\nis not a surprise given the strength of the CAA.\n\nThis Weekend - Early Next Week: Dry and Gradual Warming Trend\n\nJust how long the cold air sticks around has become more \nvariable with the newest model guidance. There are models \nshowing the upper level low retrograde back southwest towards \nthe Great Lakes this weekend, keeping the cooler airmass in \nplace for longer than previously anticipated. The main axis of \ncold air would shift off to the east, but highs may stay below \n50 through Sunday and potentially Monday before warming up\nthereafter. In any case, the upper level pattern will not be \nfavorable for precipitation chances so dry conditions are \nforecast after Thursday.",
